# Azure Artifact Signing, formerly Trusted Signing.
#
# The single place these values are written down for Revenant. Every other
# Locke Werks repository used to carry a hand-maintained metadata.json, which is
# how CertificateProfileName drifted into three different answers across four
# repositories before Forge moved to generating it. Revenant starts from the
# generated pattern rather than acquiring the same problem later.
#
# The certificate resolves to Specter Point Intelligence, LLC, the parent
# organisation. That publisher name on a Revenant binary is correct and is not
# a mis-signing.
#
# Consolidating on one profile is not tidiness. SmartScreen reputation
# accumulates against the certificate that signed a file, and EV certificates
# no longer bypass SmartScreen, so splitting releases across profiles splits the
# reputation for nothing.
#
# metadata.json.in beside this file lists nine credential types under
# ExcludeCredentials. That list is what leaves EnvironmentCredential as the only
# survivor of DefaultAzureCredential's chain, so signing authenticates with
# AZURE_TENANT_ID, AZURE_CLIENT_ID and AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET and nothing else. The
# principal is a user principal with a secret and is not federated: there is no
# workload identity and no OIDC trust relationship to configure. Without the
# exclusions the chain tries a managed identity first, waits on a metadata
# endpoint that is not there, and fails with an error that reads like a network
# problem. Keep the list intact before suspecting the credentials.
#
# Nothing is signed yet. There is no shippable binary until M3, so this file and
# the template beside it are scaffolding that CI keeps honest rather than
# configuration anything reads today. Values can be overridden by environment
# variables of the same name, which is how CI would point at a different
# profile without editing a tracked file.

REVENANT_SIGN_ENDPOINT=https://eus.codesigning.azure.net
REVENANT_SIGN_ACCOUNT=specterpoint
REVENANT_SIGN_PROFILE=specterpoint
